{{History of Okaiken|periods}}
[[File:Sengoku period battle.jpg|thumb|right|300px|A 17th-century scroll depicting the Battle of Nakagute, a symbol of the collapse of traditional authority during the Sengoku era.]]

The {{nihongo|'''Sengoku period'''|戦国時代|Sengoku jidai|extra=lit. Age of Civil War; c. 1467 – 1625}} is a period in [[History of Okaiken|Sanese history]] marked by social upheaval, political intrigue, and nearly constant military conflict that lasted roughly from the middle of the 15th century to the beginning of the 17th century. The conflict had arisen from the structural failure of the shōen estate system imposed during the late [[Asama period]]. By the time the [[Heiji period]] concluded, the control of the shōen system had been overtaken by local provincial lords, known as daimyōs, and local religious groups such as Jōdo-shinshu.

Although the [[Minato Shogunate]] retained the structure of the Imperial Bakufu and instituted a warrior government based on social economic rights and obligations established by the [[Jofun Code]], it failed to win the loyalty of some daimyō, especially those whose areas were very far from the capital of Heiji-kyō. Prominent among these dissenters were the [[Mitsui clan]] headed by [[Mitsui Terutora]] and the [[Chosokabe clan]], headed by [[Chosokabe Morihiro]]. 

As trade with the [[Kingdom of Zong]] grew and the introduction of Sanese-[[Kwangju]] trade accelerated, the economy of the archipelago developed rapidly, and many commercial cities such as [[Toyozawa]] and [[Aizawa]] were established. This, combined with developments in agriculture and small-scale trading, led to the desire for greater local autonomy throughout all levels of the social hierarchy. As early as the beginning of the 16th century, the suffering caused by earthquakes such as the Great Joyu Earthquake of 1549 and widespread famines triggered several armed uprisings by farmers.

==Origins==
{{Main|Kitayama period|Schism of the Dual Suns}}

While the Sengoku period is connected to the events that saw the decentralization of the Kitayama Shogunate, strictly speaking, the period began in 1467 with the outbreak of the Taiju war. The shogunal government of the Minato shogunate, although in power for more than a century, was quickly losing its control of the government. This was partly due to the decaying shōen system and the increasing influence of rival clans upon the imperial family, which escalated regional conflicts. 

The geopolitical landscape of the archipelago had already been fractured during the 14th century by the Schism of the Dual Suns. This bitter succession war split the Imperial line, creating a Minato-backed Northern Court in Mito (modern Okaiken) and a Southern Court in Miyagami (modern [[Nakamizu]]). While the northern territories descended toward the Sengoku civil war, the southern nation of Nakamizu experienced its own localized fragmentation known as the Sengoku Ransei under the waning [[Nogizaka Shogunate]]. Meanwhile, the [[Yanbaru]] people to the far north remained completely detached from the escalating violence, maintaining a peaceful, independent maritime kingdom that profited immensely by acting as trade intermediaries between the warring southern domains and the Fosian mainland.

Additionally, by the time the war broke out between clans in 1467, the Minato clan leadership was busy promoting the arts and culture with the imperial family, the court, and the elite, effectively degrading their control over other sectors of the government. The Minato clan did propose some changes to the shōen system, primarily through the creation of another system that favored the Minato clan allies as well as the imperial court. This was known as the {{nihongo|Ritsuryō|律令|Ritsuryō}} system. This system was a series of laws inspired by Zongese Confucianism and legalism imported from the [[Fosia|Fosian mainland]]. This resulted in the confusing complexity of Ancient Sanese law that ultimately allowed ruling military governors of the clans, such as the Ogasawara and the Chosokabe, to usurp taxation power away from the Minato Shogunate.

===Governance and Fiscal Systems===
{{Main|Jofun Code|Kandaka system|Bunkoku-hō}}

The Sengoku period was defined not merely by military conflict but by a fundamental transformation in how territory was governed and taxed. The two competing fiscal systems of the era—the old [[Jofun Code]] and the emerging {{nihongo|''kandaka''|貫高|kandaka}} system—represented opposite philosophies of statecraft.

The Jofun Code assessed wealth in ''koku'' of rice, tying a daimyō's revenue and military obligation to agricultural yield. This system functioned adequately in the fertile plains of Harujima and the Inland Sea corridor, but proved disastrous for the volcanic provinces of [[Settsu]] and the maritime domains of [[Ishijima]], where rice cultivation was unreliable. Daimyō like [[Mitsui Terutora]] and [[Chōsokabe Morihiro]] were forced to pay taxes in a currency they could scarcely produce, turning the Jofun Code from a taxation framework into an extraction mechanism. The later Ritsuryō reform, which layered Confucian bureaucratic law atop the existing shōen structure, only worsened the confusion—creating overlapping jurisdictions between shōen holders, Ritsuryō officials, and military governors that no single authority could enforce.

The kandaka system, by contrast, assessed land not by area or rice yield but by its productive capacity in coin (''kan''). This innovation—adopted by the most commercially integrated domains—allowed sengoku daimyō to monetize their territories through trade tolls, market taxes, and maritime licensing rather than relying solely on agricultural extraction. The shift from ''koku'' to ''kan'' was not merely accounting; it was a revolution in governance. Lords who adopted kandaka could raise professional standing armies paid in coin, fund castle construction on a scale the Minato Shogunate never achieved, and conduct independent diplomacy with the [[Kingdom of Zong]] and other Fosian powers. However, the system also created extreme regional inequality: provinces near trade routes—the Inland Sea, [[Toyozawa]], [[Aizawa]]—grew wealthy, while landlocked agricultural provinces stagnated, intensifying the very rivalries that fueled the war.

At the local level, governance was surprisingly decentralized. Village headmen (''muramura-gashira'') managed irrigation, dispute resolution, and tax collection, serving as the real interface between the peasantry and the daimyō. In many domains, tax collection was outsourced to local notables or merchants—a practice known as tax farming—that created a class of intermediate power brokers neither samurai nor peasant. Each major daimyō also issued their own {{nihongo|domain law codes|分国法|bunkoku-hō}}, which functioned as constitutions for their territories. These covered criminal punishment, land inheritance, military obligation, and market regulation; the most sophisticated—the [[Ogasawara House Code]] and the [[Chōsokabe Maritime Code]]—were comprehensive legal frameworks that directly challenged the Minato Shogunate's claim to be the sole source of law.

===The Imperial Court During the Sengoku Period===
{{Main|Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Caged Mikado}}

Since the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]], the Emperor of Okaiken had been a {{nihongo|"Caged Mikado"|籠の帝|Kago no Mikado}}—a ceremonial figurehead whose primary function was to confer legitimacy on whichever warlord controlled the capital. By the Sengoku period, the Imperial Court's situation had deteriorated to near-total destitution. The court's annual income had plummeted from over 1,000 ''kan'' in the pre-Taiju era to approximately 690 ''kan'', as the shōen estates that once funded the throne were seized by daimyō, pirate fleets, or religious leagues. The court could no longer fund its own security or even the materials for essential Shoto rituals.

During the Taiju War, the Shogun and Emperor famously retreated to the Silver Pavilion of Daigo-ji and ignored the destruction of the capital—a moment that became emblematic of the court's helplessness. Yet the court was not entirely passive. It survived through three strategies: **title-granting** (conferring court ranks on daimyō in exchange for financial patronage), **decree-based mediation** (issuing ''mikiri'' to mediate between warring lords, providing a face-saving mechanism for daimyō who wished to end costly conflicts), and **cultural patronage** (maintaining the court as the arbiter of aesthetic standards that even the most ruthless warlord aspired to).

[[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]], who reigned during the most critical phase of the Sengoku period, embodied this paradox of ceremonial powerlessness combined with indispensable symbolic legitimacy. The [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558 shattered the convention that the emperor's person was inviolate; the Ogasawara clan's abduction of Prince Fumihito demonstrated that even the throne's physical safety was now subject to warlord power. Throughout the Ogasawara-Takigawa period, the emperor was used as a mediator and legitimizing instrument—Ogasawara Terumasa invoked imperial decrees to justify his campaigns, while Takigawa Kazumasu sought imperial sanction for the Sword Hunt and Land Survey. The court could not refuse; its survival depended on the patronage of whoever held the capital. Emperor Komei's single most significant act came in 1601, when he publicly recognized Honda Shigetada as his abducted son Prince Fumihito—a sovereign declaration that merged military authority with divine legitimacy and created the unprecedented {{nihongo|"Dual Sovereignty"|両界の令|Ryōkai-no-Rei}}.

==Taiju War==
{{main|Taiju War}}

The first event that sparked the Sengoku period in the northern archipelago was an upheaval called the Taiju war (1467–1477). Supporters of the Minato Shogunate were caught in a bitter succession crisis between three individuals set to replace the childless Minato no Yoshimoto in Mito. They were:
* Murakami Yoshihide of Ise, a nephew of Minato no Yoshimoto through his deceased brother, Minato no Yoshikatsu.
* Sugi Yoshimasa of Seto, a monk-turned-warrior and the younger brother of Yoshimoto.
* Katsuura Masayoshi of the Saitama domain, Yoshimoto's powerful cousin.

With the sickly Yoshimoto bedridden, he issued contradictory decrees. He publicly declared that Yoshimasa should be the next shogun of the realm; but privately, he also assured supporters that Yoshihide would be his next successor. Upon his death, utter confusion arose between the three different camps. The Sugi faction argued that Yoshihide should not be the next successor to the clan, proposing instead that Yoshimasa's young son, Sumiyoshi, inherit the title. 

Katsuura Masayoshi, on the other hand, took the confusion to his advantage and launched a preemptive attack on Murakami Yoshihide at his compound in Mito, killing him. This assassination angered Yoshihide's surviving supporters, who then launched an unprecedented assault on the residence of Masayoshi in Mito, killing Masayoshi's son in the process. Masayoshi then mistakenly blamed Yoshimasa for the death of his son, and he later waged an all-out war against the Katsuura and the Sugi clans. This brutal conflict of attrition led to the total destruction of the capital [[Mito]] and the areas surrounding it, sparking a century of nationwide warfare.

==Post-Taiju War and the Era of Gekokujō==
While the streets of the capital ran with blood, the Shogun and the Emperor famously detached themselves from reality, spending the decade-long war in the secluded Silver Pavilion of Daigo-ji. When the Treaty of Natsunaga finally ended the immediate conflict in 1474, the surviving clan armies simply abandoned the depopulated ruins of the capital to return to their ancestral lands, effectively ending the Minato Shogunate's relevance.

The power vacuum left by the ruined capital birthed the phenomenon of {{nihongo|Gekokujō|下克上}}, literally translated as "the low conquers the high." With the central authority impotent, the traditional prestige of court-appointed governors evaporated. They were ruthlessly overthrown by their own deputies, capable subordinates, or charismatic peasant leaders who rejected hereditary status in favor of meritocratic martial strength. 

One of the earliest examples was [[Hōjō Sōun]], a minor rōnin who seized Izu Province in 1493 and established the [[Late Hōjō clan]]. He transformed the Kanto region into a fortress state, ruling independent of any central authority for a century. In every province, the old guard fell; the prestigious Hosokawa clan was supplanted by their vassals, the Miyoshi, and the Toki clan was overthrown by the Saitō, who were in turn destroyed by their own vassal, the peasant-born general [[Takigawa Kazumasu]].

==The Rise of Religious Militancy==
The breakdown of order also radicalized the peasantry. In the absence of samurai protection, villages formed self-defense leagues known as ikki. The most powerful of these were the Ikkō-ikki (Single-Minded Leagues), radical adherents of Jōdo Shinshū Buddhism. 

Led by warrior-monks who preached that salvation could be earned through holy war against corrupt samurai, the Ikkō-ikki seized control of entire provinces. In Kaga Province, they overthrew the local daimyō and established a "Peasant's Kingdom" that ruled itself for nearly a century. Their fortified temple-cities, such as the massive Ishiyama Hongan-ji near Aizawa, became independent states within the state, posing a formidable challenge to any warlord attempting to unify the country. This religious militancy would eventually force the unifying warlords—Ogasawara, Takigawa, and Honda—to adopt policies of brutal suppression, culminating in the burning of Mount Hiei.

==Yuzawa Incidents and the Lost Prince==
{{Main|Yuzawa Incident|Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi}}

The chaos of the period eventually violated the sanctity of the Imperial House itself, an event that would paradoxically lead to the end of the Sengoku era. On October 12, 1558, while the Imperial Family was staying at the Mikasawa summer palace in Mino province, assailants breached the walls in what is now known as the Yuzawa Incident. The attack was orchestrated by the [[Ogasawara clan]], led by Ogasawara Terunobu, who sought to extort the Imperial Court for territorial concessions. During the raid, Prince Mutsuhito was assassinated, and the young Prince Fumihito, the son of [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]], was abducted.

The Ogasawara clan used the hostage prince to demand the northern quarter of Harujima island and a stipend of 300,000 koku, which the terrified court granted. However, rather than returning the prince, the Ogasawara falsely claimed he had died of disease. In reality, the young prince was abandoned at the Shōhei Temple, where he was raised as a commoner named "Ichimatsu" under the guidance of the monk Zen no Kishō. He was eventually discovered and adopted by [[Honda Shigetatsu]], the leader of the rising Honda clan. Unaware of his true lineage, the prince took the name [[Honda Shigetada]]. He would grow up to become a fearsome general known as "The Warrior who Surpassed Death," eventually reunifying the nation and reclaiming his birthright.

===Honda Shigetatsu and the Rise of the Honda Clan===
{{Main|Honda Shigetatsu|Honda clan}}

The adoption of the lost prince by [[Honda Shigetatsu]] was not merely an act of charity—it was the defining moment that would reshape the archipelago's fate. Shigetatsu was a provincial lord who embodied the Gekokujō ethos: he had risen through merit rather than birthright, and his governance of the Honda clan reflected a philosophy that valued ability over lineage. His decision to adopt "Ichimatsu" was characteristic of the Sengoku era's social mobility—a warlord could raise a commoner, a monk, or a foundling based on perceived potential, regardless of bloodline. The supreme irony was that the boy he raised as a commoner was the imperial heir, and the meritocratic ethos of Gekokujō accidentally restored the imperial bloodline it had displaced.

Shigetatsu laid the institutional foundations that the later Honda Shogunate would build upon. He shifted the Honda clan's revenue base from rice to the kandaka coin system, enabling the clan to fund professional soldiers and purchase arquebuses. He promoted officers based on competence rather than birth—a culture that would later produce both [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] and [[Honda Shigetada]] as generals under Ogasawara Terumasa. And unlike Ogasawara Terumasa, who ruled through terror, or Takigawa Kazumasu, who ruled through fear, Shigetatsu practiced **strategic patience**: building alliances, securing supply lines, and waiting for rivals to exhaust each other. This discipline was inherited by Shigetada, who spent years consolidating before the [[Battle of Tone]], and by Shigetomo, whose administrative state bore Shigetatsu's intellectual fingerprints in every institution from the Okami Rice Exchange to the meritocratic bureaucracy.

==Fall of the Minato Clan and Regional Warlords==
By the late 16th century, the Minato Bakufu was entirely defunct, functioning merely as a puppet to whichever regional warlord occupied the ruins of the capital. During this time, ambitious daimyō sought to march on the capital to claim national hegemony. 

In 1581, the powerful warlord [[Imazato Yoshimoto]] marched his massive army toward the capital. However, he was unexpectedly annihilated by a relatively minor daimyō named [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] at the [[Battle of Mazama]]. Terumasa did not just defeat Imazato; he reportedly displayed the severed heads of 3,000 enemy samurai along the Tokaido road, issuing a grim warning that the traditional rules of honorable warfare had been replaced by a new era of absolute destruction.

==Ogasawara Clan Rises to Power==
{{main|Ogasawara Terumasa|Ogasawara clan}}
Ogasawara Terumasa spent the next decade dismantling the old medieval order with a brutality that earned him the moniker "The Demon King of the Sixth Heaven." Recognizing the threat posed by the religious leagues, Terumasa encircled their spiritual stronghold at Mount Hiei in 1584 and burned it to the ground, killing an estimated 20,000 warrior-monks and civilians. 

Having secured the capital and installed the final puppet Shogun, [[Minato Tadamoto]], Terumasa broke the economic monopolies of the ancient guilds through his free-market edicts and instituted a strict meritocracy within his ranks. He promoted brilliant generals from the peasantry, most notably [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] and [[Honda Shigetada]], expanding his territory rapidly until his sudden demise.

===The Seto Consolidation===

Before turning toward the great city of [[Toyozawa]], Terumasa secured the Seto corridor — the route between the Ogasawara's base in [[Mino Province]] and the commercial heart of Yagikoshu. The consolidation of the Seto corridor established three patterns of Ogasawara expansion that would define the clan's methods:

The '''first pattern — betrayal of submission'''. The [[Katagami clan]] of [[Abe, Seto|Abe]], headed by the young [[Katagami Minosuke]], submitted to the Ogasawara with a guarantee of territorial integrity. In 1578, Terumasa personally beheaded the surrendered lord and displayed his head for three days.

The '''second pattern — military conquest'''. The [[Iwasaki clan]] of [[Iwasaki, Seto|Iwasaki]], headed by [[Iwasaki Nobuharu]], refused to submit. After a three-week siege in 1579, the Iwasaki lord was killed in single combat by the Ogasawara retainer [[Inoue Shigetada]].

The '''third pattern — exploitation of internal unrest'''. The [[Hasegawa clan]] of [[Kurosaki]], headed by the oppressive [[Hasegawa Tadaharu]], was destroyed when the Ogasawara encouraged the [[Kurosaki Uprising]] (1579–1580) — a revolt of fishermen and merchants — and then seized the port by sea.

The three patterns were noted by [[Honda Shigetada]], who was present at all three incidents as a young general serving under Terumasa. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Shigetada's observations of the Ogasawara's methods during the Seto consolidation formed the foundation of the principles that the Honda Shogunate would later institutionalise as prohibitions.

===The Push Toward Toyozawa and the First Naval War===

With the Seto corridor secured, Terumasa turned toward the great commercial city of [[Toyozawa]] — the economic heart of Yagikoshu, ruled by the [[Onuma clan]] under [[Onuma Narishige|Narishige]]. The Onuma were the most formidable opponent the Ogasawara faced: the city was fortified, garrisoned by [[Ikkō-ikki]] warrior-monks, and supplied by sea from the [[Saitama Prefecture|Saitama]] and [[Kochi Prefecture|Kochi]] domains on the western coast of Yagikoshu.

The [[Godo Campaign]] (c. 1584–1586) saw the Ogasawara seize the town of [[Godo]] in [[Mie Prefecture|Mie Province]], closing the western overland approach to Toyozawa and surrounding the city from the south and west. But the sea route — from the port of [[Suminoe]] through the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the western coast — remained open.

The resulting [[Siege of Toyozawa (1586-1592)|Siege of Toyozawa]] (c. 1586–1592) produced the '''first major naval warfare in Okaiken's history'''. The Ogasawara dispatched a fleet to blockade Suminoe and intercept the supply convoys, but the combined naval forces of the Onuma, Saitama, and Kochi — experienced maritime clans from the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]] coast — were larger and more experienced in naval warfare. The supply convoys continued to reach Toyozawa throughout the six-year siege, and the Ogasawara were unable to take the city.

The siege was ongoing when the [[Honto-ji Incident]] of June 1592 ended the Ogasawara's campaign. With Terumasa's death, the siege was lifted, and the Onuma clan emerged as one of the principal beneficiaries of the Ogasawara's fall.

==The Honda-Takigawa Rift==
{{Main|Invasion of Kochi|Honto-ji Incident|Takigawa Kazumasu}}

The final, apocalyptic phase of the Sengoku era — classified by historians as the Seto-Tosa Period (1582–1625) — was defined not merely by the clash of armies but by a slow, invisible fracture within the Ogasawara camp itself: the growing rift between the Honda and Takigawa factions. This rift, engineered with patience and precision by [[Takigawa Kazumasu]], would ultimately reshape the archipelago more decisively than any battlefield.

===The Matsuya Post-Station Incident (1582)===
{{Main|Matsuya Post-Station Incident}}

The first crack appeared in 1582, at a post-station on the Tōkaidō road called Matsuya. [[Ogasawara Terumasa]], fresh from a campaign of devastating brutality, had ordered the execution of a popular young general whose loyalty had been beyond question. The circumstances of the killing — performed not in battle but as an act of arbitrary terror — shook the retainer corps.

Among those present was [[Hotta Muneharu]], a Honda retainer and later chronicler whose account in the ''Mitsukoji'' provides the primary record of the incident. As the company sat in silence after the execution, Muneharu whispered to the men beside him: "The Demon King has killed the one general who would have died for him."

The words were not a declaration of rebellion. They were an observation — quiet, private, and devastating. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Muneharu's whisper "traveled no further than three men that night, but it traveled to the right three." Within months, the phrase had circulated among the Honda retainers as a private maxim, a shared understanding that the Ogasawara lord's paranoia had begun to consume the very loyalty that sustained him.

The Matsuya Post-Station Incident did not create the Honda-Takigawa rift. But it marked the moment when the Honda retainers began to think of themselves as a distinct faction within the Ogasawara camp — men whose loyalty to Terumasa was tempered by the knowledge that loyalty, under the Demon King, was a death sentence.

===The Invasion of Kochi and the Execution of Gonroku (1587)===
{{Main|Invasion of Kochi|Shibata Gonroku Kazutomo}}

The rift widened decisively in 1587, with the [[Invasion of Kochi]]. [[Shibata Gonroku Kazutomo]], one of Terumasa's most reliable field commanders, conquered Kochi Province with characteristic speed and discipline. Gonroku was everything Kazumasu was not: blunt, physically powerful, a soldier's soldier who had risen through martial competence rather than administrative cunning. The two men had served together for over a decade, and their mutual contempt was an open secret in the Ogasawara camp.

Gonroku's rapid success and the adoration he earned from the rank-and-file threatened Kazumasu's position as Terumasa's most trusted general. Kazumasu responded with a campaign of systematic manipulation: he intercepted Gonroku's correspondence, reported to Terumasa that Gonroku was building a personal power base in Kochi, and fabricated reports that Gonroku had spoken of Terumasa's authority as temporary. The manipulation was designed to trigger Terumasa's deepest paranoia — and it succeeded.

In the autumn of 1587, Terumasa summoned Gonroku to the Ogasawara headquarters and executed him personally. The killing was brutal: no trial, no opportunity for seppuku, no honorable death. Gonroku was struck down while kneeling — multiple blows from behind, his sword still sheathed. The ''Mitsukoji'' describes the act as "the act of a man killing not an enemy but a fear." Gonroku's head was displayed on a spear above the gate.

The execution sent shockwaves through the retainer corps. Gonroku had been popular, competent, and — by every objective measure — loyal. His murder demonstrated that Terumasa's paranoia had become a weapon that could be wielded by anyone with access to the Demon King's ear. The Honda retainers recognized that the hand on the weapon was Kazumasu's.

===The Coalescence of the Honda Retainers===

In the weeks following Gonroku's execution, the Honda retainers began meeting privately at the quarters of [[Anjō Motonobu]], the naval commander who was also Shigetada's father-in-law. These meetings were not conspiratorial in the conventional sense — no plans were made to overthrow Terumasa, no oaths were sworn. They were, in the ''Mitsukoji''<nowiki/>'s words, "the meetings of men who had seen what a lord could do and wished to survive it."

The retainers began to coalesce around [[Honda Shigetada]] as a counterweight to Kazumasu's growing influence. Shigetada himself did not instigate this movement; the ''Mitsukoji'' is explicit that he was "the last to know what was being said in his own name." But his reputation as a general, his adoption by the meritocratic Honda Shigetatsu, and his marriage to Motonobu's daughter [[Hatsu-no-kata]] made him the natural focal point for retainers who feared the Takigawa camp's ascendancy.

===Mechanisms of the Rift===

The Honda-Takigawa rift operated through two mechanisms, both controlled by Kazumasu:

The '''first mechanism was manipulation'''. Kazumasu had inherited and expanded the Ogasawara intelligence network, and through it he controlled the flow of information to Terumasa. He could suppress reports that might favor the Honda, amplify reports that might damage them, and fabricate intelligence to order. The Honda retainers could not compete with this capability; they had no independent channel to Terumasa, and every message they sent passed through hands loyal to Kazumasu.

The '''second mechanism was silence'''. The Honda were systematically excluded from strategic decisions. Campaign plans, troop allocations, and diplomatic correspondence were routed through Kazumasu's staff, with the Honda informed only of what they needed to execute. This exclusion was not overt — Kazumasu was careful to maintain the appearance of consultation — but its effect was cumulative. By the late 1580s, the Honda had been reduced to executors rather than participants in the Ogasawara war machine.

The ''Mitsukoji'' summarizes the dynamic: "The Honda could see what was being done to them, but they could not say it aloud. To accuse Kazumasu was to accuse Terumasa's most trusted general before Terumasa himself — and Terumasa had already shown what he did to those who questioned his trust."

==The Death of Anjō Motonobu==
{{Main|Anjō Motonobu|Battle of Saikawa|First invasion of Sanzu}}

The rift's first casualty was not a Honda retainer but the man who had hosted the private meetings: [[Anjō Motonobu]] (1545–1589), the naval commander who had built the [[Anjō-Murakami Suigun]] — the fleet that gave the Ogasawara their first real naval capability. Motonobu was Shigetada's father-in-law, the father of Hatsu-no-kata, and the grandfather of the future [[Emperor Daigo]]. His death would prove to be the emotional and political turning point of the entire Sengoku period.

===The First Invasion of Sanzu (1588–1589)===

In 1588, Terumasa ordered the first invasion of [[Sanzu]], the island domain that had repelled earlier Ogasawara expeditions. The campaign required a naval force, and Motonobu — the Ogasawara camp's most experienced naval commander — was given overall command of the invasion fleet, which he assembled at the port of [[Tsuda]].

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Motonobu approached the campaign with characteristic thoroughness, building a fleet of over 200 vessels and training a marine corps capable of amphibious assault. But the campaign was undermined from the start by the Takigawa camp's control of logistics and communication. Reinforcements that Motonobu requested were delayed or redirected; intelligence reports that should have reached him were "lost" in transit; and the strategic plan that should have coordinated the naval and land components was never fully communicated to his staff.

===The Battles of Tsudanouchi and Saikawa (1589)===

The invasion force landed in Sanzu in the late summer of 1589. The [[Battle of Tsudanouchi]] in September was a defeat: Motonobu's vanguard was overwhelmed by Sanzu forces that had been alerted to the landing point — intelligence that the ''Mitsukoji'' suggests was deliberately allowed to reach the enemy. Motonobu regrouped and marched inland, but his forces were under-strength and his supply lines were precarious.

The [[Battle of Saikawa]] in October 1589 was the end. Motonobu's exhausted army, numbering perhaps 3,000, was confronted by a Sanzu force of over 10,000. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Motonobu sent a messenger to [[Honda Shigetada]] requesting urgent reinforcement — a messenger who never arrived. The message was intercepted, the ''Mitsukoji'' states, with the quiet observation: "The letter was permitted to be taken."

Motonobu died at Saikawa fighting in the vanguard, his body recovered by his retainers and carried back to the fleet. The defeat was comprehensive: of the invasion force, fewer than a third returned to Okaiken.

===The Takigawa's Engineering===

The ''Mitsukoji'' is unambiguous about the cause of Motonobu's death. The Takigawa camp engineered it through three deliberate acts:

'''First''', reinforcements were withheld. The troops that Motonobu requested — and that Terumasa had approved — were never dispatched. Kazumasu's staff attributed the delay to "logistical difficulties," a claim that the ''Mitsukoji'' dismisses as "the excuse of a man who has decided not to send what he has been ordered to send."

'''Second''', the messenger was "permitted" to be intercepted. Shigetada's urgent appeal for reinforcement was allowed to fall into enemy hands. The ''Mitsukoji'' does not accuse Kazumasu of personally intercepting the message; rather, it describes a more insidious method: the communication channel through which the message had to pass was one that Kazumasu controlled, and the security protocols that should have protected it were simply not applied. "The pen-pusher did not hold the blade at Saikawa," the ''Mitsukoji'' states. "He did not need to. He needed only to hold the letter, and to let it go at the right moment."

'''Third''', Motonobu was excluded from strategic communication. The intelligence that should have warned him of the Sanzu forces' strength and disposition was never forwarded. He fought blind, and the blindness was manufactured.

The ''Mitsukoji'' frames the killing of Motonobu as the second act in Kazumasu's campaign against the Honda — the first having been the execution of Gonroku, the second being the death of Motonobu, and the third being the Honto-ji Incident itself. "Gonroku was killed to show what the Demon King could do," the chronicle states. "Motonobu was killed to show what the pen-pusher could do. The one was a murder of passion; the other was a murder of calculation."

===Shigetada's Grief===

The death of Motonobu devastated Shigetada. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that when news of Saikawa reached him, Shigetada withdrew to his quarters for three days and was not seen by his retainers. When he emerged, he was, in the words of Hotta Muneharu, "a man remade — not harder, but deeper, as if the grief had carved a chamber in him that had not existed before."

The ''Mitsukoji'' describes Shigetada's grief with a precision that has become one of the most quoted passages in the chronicle: "This was not the grief of a general who has lost a commander. It was the grief of a man who has lost a father, and who knows who killed him."

The phrase "and who knows who killed him" is the ''Mitsukoji''<nowiki/>'s first explicit accusation of Kazumasu's responsibility for a death. It marks the moment when the Honda retainers' private understanding became, in the chronicle's narrative, a certainty — and when Shigetada's opposition to Kazumasu ceased to be political and became personal.

==The Hidden Identity==
{{Main|Yuzawa Incident|Honda Shigetada|Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi}}

At some point between 1587 and 1590 — the ''Mitsukoji'' does not specify the date, and the [[Honda Letters]] declassified in 2016 provide only fragments — Kazumasu discovered Shigetada's true identity as [[Prince Fumihito]], the son of [[Emperor Komei]] abducted in the [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558. The discovery was made through the Ogasawara intelligence network, which had been tracing the fate of the abducted prince as a matter of state security.

The discovery gave Kazumasu a weapon of unprecedented power — one that could be wielded against two targets simultaneously.

===The Weapon Against Terumasa===

Kazumasu used the identity to undermine Terumasa's trust in Shigetada. In private audiences with the Demon King, Kazumasu suggested that the Honda general's popularity and military brilliance were not merely assets but potential threats: "The Honda general has a blood claim that no battlefield can settle." The implication was that Shigetada, as the abducted imperial prince, possessed a legitimacy that rivaled — and potentially superseded — Terumasa's own authority. Terumasa, whose paranoia was already a defining characteristic, did not need to be told twice.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Terumasa's behavior toward Shigetada changed subtly after Kazumasu's revelation. The Demon King became more distant, more guarded, and increasingly inclined to assign Shigetada to campaigns that would keep him away from the capital. The siege of Toyozawa, which consumed Shigetada's attention from 1586 to 1592, served Kazumasu's purposes perfectly: it kept the Honda general occupied, distant, and unable to monitor the growing influence of the Takigawa camp.

===The Weapon Against Shigetada===

Against Shigetada himself, Kazumasu employed a more direct method. A private message was delivered to the Honda general — the ''Mitsukoji'' does not record its exact wording, but its import was clear: "The prince's health is a matter of state."

The message was a leash. It informed Shigetada that Kazumasu knew his identity, and that this knowledge could be used at any moment — disclosed to Terumasa (who might order his execution as a potential rival), announced publicly (which would destroy the Honda clan's autonomy by making Shigetada a ward of the Imperial Court), or used as leverage in any future political confrontation. The phrase "a matter of state" was particularly devastating: it framed the knowledge not as a personal secret but as a political instrument, one that Kazumasu controlled.

The ''Mitsukoji'' describes the effect with characteristic precision: "Shigetada, who had faced cavalry charges without flinching, who had walked through the fire of Toyozawa's walls without hesitation, could not act against the man who held his name. The blade that could not touch him on the battlefield had been placed against his throat by a letter."

The hidden identity paralyzed Shigetada at the critical moment. When the Honto-ji Incident came in 1592, the Honda general — who might have intervened, who might have positioned his forces to protect Terumasa, who might have challenged Kazumasu's growing power — did nothing. He could not. The man who held his name held him still.

==The Honto-ji Incident==
{{Main|Honto-ji Incident}}

The betrayal of Ogasawara Terumasa on the night of June 21, 1592, was the culmination of Kazumasu's five-year campaign — a campaign that had begun with the execution of Gonroku in 1587, continued through the engineered death of Motonobu in 1589, and reached its climax in the burning of Honto-ji Temple in Mito.

===The Betrayal===

Terumasa had retired to [[Honto-ji Temple]] in [[Mito]], preparing to travel north to suppress a reported uprising in Yagikoshu. His bodyguard detail was minimal: thirty trusted retainers. The temple, a center of Jōdo-shū Baiduism, had long served as a retreat for the Ogasawara leadership.

At approximately midnight, the temple's outer gates were opened from within by agents who had infiltrated the monastic staff over the preceding months. Kazumasu's forces — over 1,000 men carrying torches and oil — poured into the compound. Their orders were explicit: leave no survivors.

The attack unfolded with terrifying efficiency. Terumasa's bodyguards were overwhelmed within minutes. The temple buildings were set alight, and those who attempted to flee the flames were cut down by musketry positioned at every exit. Terumasa himself, awakened by the commotion, was shot by an arquebusier positioned on the temple's roof. According to the most reliable accounts, his last words were addressed to Kazumasu, who had entered the temple to witness his lord's end: "I raised a serpent in my bosom."

The fire consumed Honto-ji entirely. Over 200 people died in the attack, including Terumasa, his nephew [[Ogasawara Hidetsugu]], and numerous senior Ogasawara retainers. The bodies were burned beyond recognition, and Kazumasu would later claim that Terumasa had perished attempting to escape.

===The Honda's Absence===

The Honda forces were three days' march from Mito when Honto-ji burned. They had been positioned there by Kazumasu's manipulation — assigned to the eastern front of the ongoing Toyozawa siege, a posting that kept them distant from the capital and unable to respond to any event in Mito. The ''Mitsukoji'' states: "The Honda were exactly where Kazumasu had placed them: far enough to be absent, close enough to be blamed."

Shigetada's paralysis was total. The hidden identity — the leash that Kazumasu had fastened years earlier — held him still at the moment when action might have saved his lord. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that Shigetada received news of the attack two days after it occurred and "sat without speaking for the rest of the day, his retainers afraid to approach him." The chronicle does not describe him weeping, raging, or issuing orders. It describes silence — the silence of a man who knew that the death of his lord had been engineered by the same hand that held his name, and who could not act against either.

===Aftermath===

[[Ogasawara Hidemitsu]], a distant cousin of Terumasa, rallied a force of 8,000 loyalists in Owari Province and marched on the capital. The [[Battle of Shizugatake]] in August 1592 ended in a decisive victory for Kazumasu's forces, commanded by his general [[Yamagata Masakage]]. Hidemitsu was captured and executed, and his head was displayed at the gates of [[Okami]] alongside those of his principal retainers.

By the end of 1592, Kazumasu had secured control of the central provinces and had been recognized by the Imperial Court as ''[[Kampaku]]'' (Imperial Regent). The era of the Takigawa Terror had begun.

==The Takigawa Terror (1592–1600)==
{{Main|Takigawa Terror|Council of Four Elders|Takigawa Kazumasu}}

The eight years between the Honto-ji Incident and Kazumasu's death at the [[Siege of Katsuura]] on June 18, 1600 are known retrospectively as the {{nihongo|"Takigawa Terror"|滝川の恐怖|Takigawa no Kyōfu}}. The period was defined by two modes of governance: '''manipulation''' — the use of intelligence networks, communication control, and engineered betrayals to neutralize opposition — and '''compliance''' — the imposition of administrative systems so total that resistance became structurally impossible.

===The Council of Four Elders===

Kazumasu governed through the {{nihongo|"Council of Four Elders"|四老評定|Yonrō Hyōtei}}, an administrative body that concentrated authority in four individuals: Kazumasu himself, who retained supreme command; [[Sawano Hiroyuki]], the chief administrator; [[Yamagata Masakage]], the supreme military commander; and [[Maeda Toshiie]], the minister of security. This arrangement allowed Kazumasu to project an image of collective leadership while maintaining ultimate control over all significant decisions.

The council met formally each morning in the Great Hall of [[Okami Castle]], but in practice, most decisions were made in private audiences between Kazumasu and individual elders, with Sawano serving as the gatekeeper who controlled access to the supreme ruler.

===Compliance: Land Survey, Sword Hunt, and the Four-Class System===

Kazumasu's most enduring achievements were administrative. The [[Land Survey]] (''Taikō Kenchi''), implemented between 1593 and 1598, was the first comprehensive cadastral assessment of the entire archipelago. Teams of surveyors measured every cultivated field, classified land by productivity, and recorded ownership in standardized registers. The survey identified approximately 15% of previously unreported cultivated land and brought it under central taxation.

The [[Sword Hunt]] (''Katanagari''), conducted in 1594 and 1595, forcibly disarmed the peasantry. Kazumasu's agents collected swords, spears, and firearms from anyone unable to prove samurai status. The stated justification was religious — the weapons would be melted down to construct a Great Buddha statue — but the real purpose was to eliminate the possibility of armed peasant rebellion and to enforce a rigid social hierarchy.

The [[four-class system]] (士農工商, ''shi-nō-kō-shō'') codified the population into hereditary categories: samurai, farmers, artisans, and merchants. Movement between classes was strictly prohibited. The system froze the social mobility that had defined the earlier Gekokujō era — the same mobility that had allowed a peasant's son like Kazumasu himself to rise to supreme power.

===The Illegal Ishijima Invasion (1590)===

Even before the Honto-ji Incident, Kazumasu had demonstrated his willingness to act without authorization. In 1590, he launched an invasion of [[Ishijima]] from the Seto naval base — without Terumasa's knowledge or consent. The invasion served three purposes: it dispersed Honda strength by drawing forces to the eastern front; it gave the Takigawa camp control of Seto's naval resources; and it created a pool of Takigawa-loyal troops who would form the backbone of Kazumasu's post-Honto-ji military.

The ''Mitsukoji'' identifies the Ishijima invasion as the first clear evidence that Kazumasu was operating independently of Terumasa — "not as a retainer who had exceeded his authority, but as a ruler who had not yet announced his rule."

===The Second Invasion of Sanzu: Deliberate Inefficiency===

Under the Takigawa regime, a second invasion of Sanzu was launched — but the ''Mitsukoji'' describes it as running with "deliberate inefficiency." The campaign was not intended to conquer Sanzu; it was intended to occupy the Honda-aligned generals who might otherwise have challenged Kazumasu's rule, to exhaust potential opposition in a distant and difficult campaign, and to maintain the fiction that the Ogasawara-Takigawa state was still pursuing Terumasa's expansionist vision.

Troops were supplied inadequately, reinforcements were delayed, and strategic objectives were never clearly defined. The campaign ground on without resolution, consuming men and resources that could not be directed against the Takigawa regime.

===The Mashita Appointment===

[[Mashita Morihiro]] was positioned by Kazumasu as a bridge between the Honda and Takigawa camps — a trusted intermediary who could communicate with both sides and mediate disputes. As a capable administrator who had proven himself during the Land Survey, Mashita was entrusted with increasingly sensitive responsibilities, culminating in his appointment as guardian of the infant heir Kazutoyo.

What Kazumasu did not fully perceive — or chose to ignore — was Mashita's deep ambivalence about the Takigawa regime. Mashita had been a loyal retainer of Ogasawara Terumasa, and he viewed Kazumasu's betrayal at the Honto-ji Incident with private horror. His subsequent service under Kazumasu was driven by pragmatism and a sense of duty to the infant heir, not by loyalty to the usurper. When Kazumasu died at Katsuura, Mashita assumed the role of regent and commanded the Western Army — not as a schemer, but as a man bound by duty to a child he had been appointed to protect.

===Grand Schemes: Okami Castle and Daigo-ji===

Kazumasu's regime was characterized by grandiose construction projects that served both political and symbolic purposes. The construction of [[Okami Castle]] — a fortress of unprecedented scale — was designed to project the Takigawa regime's permanence and power. The rebuilding of [[Daigo-ji]] — the temple where the emperor and shogun had sheltered during the Taiju War — was intended to symbolize the restoration of order under Kazumasu's patronage.

Both projects consumed vast resources and labor, drawing on the corvée labor system that the Land Survey had made possible. The ''Mitsukoji'' notes that the construction projects served an additional, unspoken purpose: they kept potentially restive samurai occupied and dependent on the regime's patronage.

===The 1598 Rejection===

In 1598, Kazumasu petitioned the Imperial Court for the title of ''Sei-i Taishōgun'' — the supreme military commander's title that would have formalized his rule. The court refused. The stated reason was that Kazumasu was peasant-born: the four-class system that he himself had codified — the system that elevated samurai above all other classes and restricted the highest offices to those of samurai birth — barred him from the title he sought.

The ''Mitsukoji'' frames the rejection as the supreme irony of Kazumasu's career: "The serpent who whispered in every ear did not hear the whisper in his own. He had built the cage that would hold him, and he had built it with the care of a man who believed he would never be inside it."

The rejection was a political earthquake. It demonstrated that the Takigawa regime, for all its power, lacked the legitimacy that only blood could confer. And it pointed — as if by invisible design — toward the one man who possessed both the military power and the blood claim: Honda Shigetada, the hidden prince.

===The Siege of Katsuura and Kazumasu's Death===
{{Main|Siege of Katsuura}}

The rejection of 1598 marked the beginning of the end for Kazumasu's regime. Seeking to reassert his military authority after the political humiliation, Kazumasu launched a campaign in the spring of 1600 to subdue the [[Nitta clan]], an independent local clan based on the western coast of [[Harujima]]. The Nitta clan had resisted Takigawa authority throughout the Terror, refusing to submit to the Land Survey or the Sword Hunt and maintaining their autonomy as a regional power.

Kazumasu laid siege to the Nitta clan's stronghold at [[Katsuura Castle]], located in what is now [[Ozu, Okazaki Prefecture|Ozu]], [[Okazaki Prefecture]], on the western coast of Harujima. The siege began in the late spring of 1600, and Kazumasu personally oversaw the operations, confident that the castle would fall as easily as the many fortresses he had reduced in the past.

However, on June 18, 1600, Kazumasu was killed by a sniper shot fired from behind — not from the castle walls, but from within his own siege lines. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that the shot was fired by a Honda assassin embedded in Kazumasu's camp during his inspection of the siege lines. Kazumasu, who had survived a decade of intrigue and betrayal, died instantly — the master of manipulation undone by a single bullet from a clan he had dismissed as a minor threat.

==The Nagai Clan and the Dead Serpent's Venom==
{{Main|Nagai clan|Invasion of Nakamizu (1603)}}

Kazumasu's capacity for manipulation did not end with his death at the [[Siege of Katsuura]] on June 18, 1600. The ''Mitsukoji'' records that as early as 1598, Kazumasu's intelligence network had identified a military buildup by the [[Nagai clan]] of southern Yagikoshu — a clan that had declared independence following Honda Shigetada's campaign against the Mitsui in 1596. Kazumasu recognized the Nagai as a potential weapon: a force that could threaten the southern coast of Harujima and draw Honda attention and resources away from the coming confrontation with the Takigawa.

Rather than suppressing the Nagai buildup, Kazumasu encouraged it — through intermediaries who fed the Nagai information about the weakness of the southern defenses and the preoccupation of the Honda with the northern front. Simultaneously, he arranged for a warning to be delivered to the [[Kawaei clan]] of southern Nakamizu through a [[Sea Wolf]] back channel — a warning that was designed not to prevent the Nagai invasion but to ensure that the Kawaei would be prepared to destroy it.

The result was the [[Nagai invasion of Nakamizu]] in 1603 — three years after Kazumasu's death and two years after the establishment of Dual Sovereignty. [[Nagai Tsunetora]] and approximately 4,000 warriors crossed the strait to Nakamizu, where they were met by the prepared forces of the Kawaei and their Kurokawa allies. The Nagai were destroyed.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records Shigetada's reaction to the news: "The dead serpent's venom still flows." The phrase captures the essential nature of Kazumasu's method — a method so deeply embedded in the structures of state, intelligence, and manipulation that it continued to function after the man who created it was gone. The Nagai invasion was the last act of the Takigawa Terror, executed by a hand that had been cold for three years.

==The Battle of Tone==
{{Main|Battle of Tone}}

The death of Kazumasu at the [[Siege of Katsuura]] on June 18, 1600, triggered the final and bloodiest conflict of the Sengoku era. The archipelago fractured into two armed camps: the [[Eastern Army]], led by Honda Shigetada and the Honda coalition; and the [[Western Army]], loyal to the infant [[Takigawa Kazutoyo]] and commanded by [[Mashita Morihiro]] as regent.

The two armies met at the [[Tone River]] on October 21, 1600. The engagement involved over 160,000 men — the largest battle in Sanese history. The Western Army held a strong defensive position, and the initial Honda assault was repelled with heavy casualties.

The battle turned on the defection of [[Koyama Hidemoto]]. Commanding a division positioned on the Western Army's flank, Koyama had been secretly negotiating with Honda Shigetada throughout the campaign. At the critical moment, Koyama's 15,000 troops descended from [[Tenjinyama]] into the Western Army's rear, shattering the Western position. The Western Army, commanded by [[Mashita Morihiro]] as regent for the infant Kazutoyo, collapsed.

The slaughter was catastrophic. Over 60,000 men died at Tone — approximately 58,000 from the Western Army and 41,000 from the Eastern. The ''Mitsukoji'' describes the battlefield afterward as "a landscape of the dead, where the living walked as if through a country that had been emptied by a plague rather than a war."

Mashita was captured several days after the battle while attempting to flee to Ise Province. Brought before Honda Shigetada as the defeated commander of the Western Army, he was executed for leading the armed opposition against the crown. Shigetada reportedly stated: "A man who raises his sword against the throne forfeits his right to speak of loyalty." Mashita was beheaded at the [[Rokumangawa]] dry riverbed on November 3, 1600.

The Battle of Tone was Honda Shigetada's victory — but it was a pyrrhic one. The human cost was staggering, and the political settlement that followed would be shaped by the need to prevent such a catastrophe from ever recurring.

==The Resolution: Dual Sovereignty and the End of the Sengoku==

===Recognition of Prince Fumihito (1601)===

In January 1601, the ailing [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]] publicly recognized Honda Shigetada as his abducted son, [[Prince Fumihito]]. The declaration was a sovereign act of unprecedented significance: it merged military authority with divine legitimacy, and it rendered the hidden identity — the weapon that Kazumasu had wielded for a decade — into a source of supreme power.

The recognition was, in one sense, the end of the Yuzawa Incident: the prince abducted in 1558 was restored to his birthright forty-three years later. In another sense, it was the beginning of a new political order. Emperor Komei and Shigetada worked out a solution together: Shigetada was declared both Crown Prince and ''Sei-i Taishōgun'', but he had no desire to join the imperial family as a courtier. He was a warrior, not a politician — and the court was a world he had never known. The compromise was the unprecedented {{nihongo|"Dual Sovereignty"|両界の令|Ryōkai-no-Rei}} — a system in which the imperial and military authorities were vested in a single person, but with distinct functions: as prince, Shigetada possessed the divine legitimacy of the imperial line; as shogun, he wielded the military authority of the warrior government. When Komei died in 1610 and Shigetada ascended as Emperor Shirakawa, he enacted the [[Edict of the Two Realms]] in 1611, formally separating the two spheres and transferring the Shogunate to his second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]].

The hidden identity ceased to be a weapon. The leash that Kazumasu had fastened — "The prince's health is a matter of state" — was dissolved by the one act that Kazumasu had not anticipated: the emperor's voluntary recognition of his son. The man who had been paralyzed by the knowledge of his own name was freed by the public declaration of it.

===The Kinzō Incident (1625)===

The Sengoku period did not end with the Battle of Tone or the establishment of Dual Sovereignty. The final act came in 1625, with the [[Kinzō Incident]] — the tragic culmination of the unresolved tensions that the era had created.

[[Ii Naotaka]], the son of [[Ii Naomasa]] — the "Red Devil" who had been one of Shigetada's most trusted generals — attacked [[Toyozawa Castle]] in a dispute over domain boundaries and succession rights. In the attack, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]] — the reign name of Honda Shigetada, who had ascended the throne in 1610 — was killed. Shigetada's son, Shogun [[Honda Shigetomo]], died at the [[Siege of Ichijoudani]] while suppressing the revolt.

The Kinzō Incident was the iron crust cracking: the ''[[tetsukage]]'' — the iron shadow that conceals soft silt beneath a hard crust — that the [[Mythology of Okaiken|Stone Pounding Cycle]] had warned of. The Sengoku had created the iron crust of the warlord system, but the unresolved problems beneath it — the loyalty of the samurai class, the relationship between the imperial and military authorities, the tension between merit and birth — broke through at Kinzō.

The resulting [[Great Reconciliation]], combined with the looming external threat of the [[Kawaei clan]] in southern Nakamizu, forced the remaining daimyō to accept the rigid, authoritarian peace of the newly established [[Honda Shogunate]]. The Sengoku period — 158 years of civil war that had begun with the Taiju War in 1467 and had been shaped by the manipulation of Takigawa Kazumasu, the loyalty of Honda Shigetada, and the tragic irony of a hidden prince — was finally over.

==Society and religion==
{{Main|Society during the Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Pure Land Baiduism|Mythology of Okaiken}}

The Sengoku period reshaped every layer of Sanese society. The warrior class was consumed by a conflict that eroded the old codes of honorable warfare and replaced them with a betrayal culture in which loyalty became a calculation rather than a covenant. The common people — peasants, fishermen, merchants — lived in the path of armies that took what they wanted and left what they could not carry. The [[kandaka system]] monetized the relationship between lord and peasant, enriching provinces near trade routes while extracting beyond capacity from landlocked agricultural domains. The hostage system destroyed families as a matter of course: children of allied or subordinate clans were raised in the castles of their enemies, forging bonds and traumas that would define the next generation.

The era's spiritual crisis transformed [[Pure Land Baiduism]] from a populist movement into both a weapon of rebellion and an instrument of state. The "Easy Path" doctrine — teaching that in the degenerate age, individual effort was insufficient for salvation and one must rely on the Vow of the Sovereign — offered grace in a world that offered none. The [[Ikkō-ikki]] radicalized this hope into alternative governance: the "Peasant's Kingdom" in Kaga Province functioned as a theocratic confederacy for nearly a century, proving that commoners could govern without a hereditary aristocracy. The destruction of the Ikkō-ikki by the unifying warlords and the subsequent "Sword and Sutra" strategy of [[Honda Shigetada]] completed the transformation: the faith that had once armed peasants became the faith of the Emperor.

The [[Mythology of Okaiken|Stone Pounding Cycle]] provided the cosmological framework through which the era was understood. The three rhythms — the Pounding (warfare), the Loosening (institutional collapse), and the Release (unification and its costs) — mapped directly onto the lived experience of 150 years of war. The [[Tesshin-shinkō|Iron Cycle]] ''emerged'' during the Sengoku as the warrior class codified their ethos into a religious framework, producing the tanren discipline and the Niō guardians that would later be mapped onto the [[Dual Sovereignty]] system. The concept of the ''[[tetsukage]]'' — the iron shadow that conceals soft silt beneath a hard crust — was the mythology's most prescient contribution: the Sengoku created the iron crust of the warlord system, but the unresolved problems beneath it would break through at the [[Kinzō Incident]].

Okaiken's Sengoku was distinguished from comparable periods of civil conflict on the Fosian mainland by its fundamentally maritime character. The [[Anjō-Murakami Suigun]] — the naval force built by [[Anjō Motonobu]] and continued by [[Murakami Motosuke]] — was the era's most significant institutional innovation, and the sea lanes that connected the archipelago to [[Yanbaru]], [[Zong]], and [[Nakamizu]] were the lifelines through which trade, pirates, and displaced warriors flowed. While Okaiken bled, Yanbaru profited as a trade intermediary, Zong expanded its colonial empire, and Nakamizu developed along an independent trajectory — and the external threat of these maritime powers shaped the Sengoku's end as decisively as the internal exhaustion.

==Clans and Territories==
{{See also|List of daimyō of the Sengoku period|Fudai|Tozama}}

The Sengoku period fragmented the archipelago among dozens of competing clans, each governing their domains as de facto independent states. The major powers were distributed across the three islands as follows:

===Harujima===
The northernmost and most commercially developed island was the primary theater of the late Sengoku and Seto-Tosa periods. The [[Late Hōjō clan]], established by [[Hōjō Sōun]]'s seizure of Mino Province in 1493, ruled the Okazaki fortress region independently for a century. The [[Kojima clan]] controlled northern Harujima; Shigetada's secret truce with the Kojima secured his northern flank before the Battle of Tone. The [[Ii clan]], based in central Harujima, earned the nickname "The Red Devils" for their ferocity under [[Ii Naomasa]]; they became the vanguard of the Honda army after Shigetada's peace settlement, and their later revolt under [[Ii Naotaka]] would culminate in the Kinzō Incident.

===Ishijima===
The central island was the stronghold of the [[Ogasawara clan]], who had originally displaced the [[Uzumaki clan]] from the Masaki Peninsula in a Chain-Link War during the Hiraizumi-Daishin period. Under Ogasawara Terunobu and Terumasa, the clan expanded its territorial base dramatically across Ishijima and into Harujima. The [[Chōsokabe clan]], maritime lords of the southern Ishijima coast under [[Chōsokabe Morihiro]], were among the earliest and most persistent dissenters against the Minato Shogunate's rice-based taxation; they surrendered to the Honda in 1610 following a naval blockade. The [[Asakura clan]] and [[Azai clan]] of northern Ishijima were allies of the Ikkō-ikki who were destroyed by Ogasawara Terumasa.

===Yagikoshu===
The volcanic southern island was the domain of the [[Mitsui clan]], headed by [[Mitsui Terutora]], whose dissent from the Minato Shogunate was driven by the Jofun Code's rice-based taxation of non-rice-producing territories. The [[Shimazu clan]] of southern Yagikoshu surrendered to the Honda-Shogunate in 1612, two years after the Chōsokabe. The [[Hōsō clan]] of northern Harujima held out until 1618, when combined Imperial-Shogunate forces completed the military reunification.

The Ogasawara clan's homeland was in [[Mino Province]] — the mountainous interior of central Yagikoshu, now divided between [[Mito Prefecture]] and [[Toyozawa Metropolis]]. The [[Onuma clan]] of [[Toyozawa]] was the most formidable opponent the Ogasawara faced on the island, withstanding a six-year siege (c. 1586–1592) through Ikkō-Ikki alliance and naval supply from the western domains. The [[Miyamori clan]] of [[Kasagi]], guardians of the [[Mikasawa Imperial Summer Palace]], were destroyed by the Ogasawara in 1557 as a precursor to the [[Yuzawa Incident]]. The [[Nagayama Confederation]] — a loose alliance of fourteen mountain lords in the Nagayama interior — maintained independence until [[Honda Shigetoki]]'s winter campaign of 1589.

===Trans-Regional Powers===
The [[Ikkō-ikki]] were not a clan but a theocratic confederation that controlled Kaga Province, the Ishiyama Hongan-ji near Aizawa, and Nagashima in Ise through a network of fortress-temples that spanned all three islands. The [[Kotsuna clan]] and the maritime branches of the [[Taira clan]] operated as pirate-subsidizers from the Inland Sea of Mitoyagi, fielding private naval forces that the Minato Shogunate was powerless to suppress. The [[Minato clan]], the former ruling house, was reduced to puppet status by the late 16th century; the final puppet Shogun, [[Minato Tadamoto]], was installed by Ogasawara Terumasa.

===Post-Unification Classification===
After the [[Battle of Tone]] (1600) and the establishment of the Honda Shogunate, the surviving clans were categorized by their loyalty to the new regime. The {{nihongo|''fudai''|譜代|fudai}} (hereditary vassals) who had supported the Honda before Tone—such as the Ii, Hotta, and Okudaira—were granted strategic domains in central Harujima and key government posts. The {{nihongo|''tozama''|外様|tozama}} (outsider lords) who submitted only after unification—including the Shimazu, Chōsokabe, and many former Ogasawara vassals—were relegated to the distant provinces of Settsu and the Ostrovs. The Ii clan, following the Kinzō Incident and Naotaka's seppuku, were pardoned but demoted to the smaller domain of Kozuka, where they served as a vital buffer against potential southern aggression for the next 250 years.

